(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-dermatology-ii-quiz"]={"title":"Dermatology II Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which variant of granuloma annulare carries the strongest systemic associations?","choices":["Localised disease, which predominates at about 75% of cases and affects children and young adults","Subcutaneous, occurring almost exclusively in children under six","Perforating, with central umbilication and extrusion of collagen","Generalised or disseminated, associated with diabetes, thyroid disease, dyslipidaemia and lymphoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is more common in adults over forty. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about patients with Fitzpatrick types IV to VI?","choices":["They are not immune to ultraviolet damage or skin cancer, and melanoma is frequently diagnosed at advanced stages","They carry no meaningful skin cancer risk and need no routine photoprotection","They burn more readily than types I to III but tan more deeply afterwards","They should use SPF 15 rather than SPF 30, on the basis that higher factors confer no additional benefit"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — delayed diagnosis is common because of a lower index of suspicion. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"What causes epidermolysis bullosa?","choices":["Autoantibodies against the epidermal basement membrane","A mutation in structural proteins","Immunoglobulin A deposition in the dermal papillae","A type IV hypersensitivity reaction to a drug"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the level at which the skin separates follows from which protein is affected. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"How broad is the referral requirement in epidermolysis bullosa?","choices":["Essentially every specialty, given the multisystem burden","Dermatology alone, since the disease is confined to the skin","Genetics alone, since the diagnosis is made on a genetic panel","No routine referral, since the condition is managed in primary care"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — palliative care, genetics and family support are all involved. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"How does polymorphous light eruption typically present?","choices":["Painful erythema developing within minutes of ultraviolet exposure and resolving within an hour of moving into the shade","An exaggerated sunburn on first exposure to a new medication","Persistent eczematous change in chronically exposed skin of older males","Papules appearing thirty minutes to hours after exposure on the décolletage, forearms and dorsal hands, sparing chronically exposed skin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, resolving in seven to ten days without scarring and recurring each spring. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"Why does azelaic acid need care in rosacea despite being effective?","choices":["It is contraindicated in pregnancy because of teratogenic risk","It raises intraocular pressure with prolonged facial application","It is more drying, and these patients already have an impaired skin barrier and dry skin","It causes marked photosensitivity, so daily sun protection becomes mandatory"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — that is why metronidazole is preferred first. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What is the mortality of toxic epidermal necrolysis, and what predicts it at presentation?","choices":["Up to 30 to 35%, predicted by the SCORTEN score calculated within 24 hours","Between 1 and 5%, predicted by the extent of mucosal involvement","Under 1%, predicted by the number of drugs the patient was taking","Around 50%, predicted by the presence of the Nikolsky sign"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and SCORTEN is repeated on day three. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"What triggers the majority of erythema multiforme cases?","choices":["Mycoplasma pneumoniae, accounting for over 50% of cases","Idiopathic causes, accounting for over 50% of cases","Herpes simplex virus, accounting for over 50% of cases","Sulfonamide antibiotics, accounting for over 50% of cases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Other infectious causes include Mycoplasma pneumoniae, histoplasmosis, Epstein-Barr virus and coxsackievirus. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What mechanism underlies the most common form of acanthosis nigricans?","choices":["Autoantibody deposition along the epithelial basement membrane zone","Hyperinsulinaemia stimulating keratinocyte and fibroblast proliferation through insulin-like growth factor 1 receptor cross-activation","A paraneoplastic process driven by an underlying gastric adenocarcinoma secreting growth factors that act on the epidermis","A delayed type IV hypersensitivity reaction to a contacted allergen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the insulin-resistant or benign form is the commonest. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which measures are named for acute sunburn?","choices":["Silver sulfadiazine dressings applied twice daily under occlusion","Cool compresses, an early nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug, and oral hydration","Warm compresses, an oral antihistamine, and a topical corticosteroid cream","Ice packs applied directly, with a systemic corticosteroid taper"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — ibuprofen or naproxen reduce the prostaglandin-mediated pain. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"What is the principal risk a patient must understand before endoscopic thoracic sympathectomy?","choices":["Compensatory sweating elsewhere, occurring in over 30%","The need for retreatment every three to six months","Dry mouth and urinary retention as ongoing side effects","Permanent destruction of axillary glands by microwave energy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the procedure is permanent, so the trade-off has to be discussed thoroughly beforehand. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which biologic is approved for refractory chronic urticaria?","choices":["Dupilumab, an interleukin-4 receptor antagonist given subcutaneously","Etanercept, a tumour necrosis factor inhibitor given subcutaneously","Omalizumab, an anti-immunoglobulin E antibody given every four weeks","Infliximab, a tumour necrosis factor alpha inhibitor given intravenously"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, at 300 mg subcutaneously. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is pyoderma gangrenosum, despite its name?","choices":["A neutrophilic dermatosis that is not infectious","A deep bacterial infection requiring prolonged antibiotics","A vasculitis of medium-sized vessels with immune complex deposition","A malignant ulcerating tumour of keratinocyte origin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — dysregulated innate immune activation with neutrophil recruitment causes the tissue destruction. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What must be checked before starting dapsone for dermatitis herpetiformis?","choices":["Thyroid-stimulating hormone and anti-thyroid peroxidase antibodies","Serum immunoglobulin A level to exclude selective deficiency","HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8 typing to confirm susceptibility","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase status"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the blood count is then monitored for haemolytic anaemia and methaemoglobinaemia. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How is a positive photopatch test interpreted?","choices":["Any reaction indicates phototoxicity regardless of which patch responds","A reaction only on the irradiated patch indicates photoallergy; a reaction on both indicates contact allergy","A reaction on both patches indicates photoallergy; a reaction on one indicates phototoxicity","A reaction on the non-irradiated patch alone indicates photoallergy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the suspected photohaptens are applied in duplicate and one set irradiated with ultraviolet A. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"Which epidermolysis bullosa subtype carries the highest mortality?","choices":["Epidermolysis bullosa acquisita, the autoimmune acquired form","Junctional epidermolysis bullosa, especially the Herlitz subtype","Epidermolysis bullosa simplex, especially the localised palmoplantar form","Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa, especially the dominant form"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is why early palliative care integration is appropriate in severe Herlitz disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What causes phytophotodermatitis, and how does it present?","choices":["Furanocoumarins in plants such as lime, celery and parsley plus ultraviolet A, producing linear streaked hyperpigmentation","Bergapten in bergamot oil used in fragrances and cosmetics, producing drip-pattern hyperpigmentation of the neck","A persistent eczematous eruption in chronically exposed skin of older males","A delayed hypersensitivity response to an ultraviolet-induced photoantigen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — classically after lime juice and sun exposure, with painful blistering acutely. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"Which human leukocyte antigen haplotypes are strongly associated with dermatitis herpetiformis?","choices":["HLA-B*15:02 and HLA-B*58:01","HLA-B27 and HLA-DR4","HLA-Cw6 and HLA-DR3","HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the same haplotypes that underlie coeliac disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Where is the eruption of dermatitis herpetiformis distributed?","choices":["On the anterior shins bilaterally as tender nodules that do not ulcerate","On the face in the central third, sparing the nasolabial folds","On sun-exposed skin, sparing the chronically exposed face and hands","Symmetrically on the knees, elbows, buttocks and back"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — intensely pruritic papules and vesicles that group in a herpetiform pattern. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which services should a patient with Stevens-Johnson syndrome be under?","choices":["Intensive care or a burn unit, with dermatology involved","A general medical ward with dermatology review the following day","Outpatient dermatology with a review appointment in one week","Ambulatory wound care with daily dressing changes at home"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — nasogastric feeding is often needed because mucosal involvement prevents eating. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"Which features should raise suspicion of secondary rather than primary hyperhidrosis?","choices":["Bilateral palmar sweating that has been present since adolescence","Sweating that worsens with anxiety and improves in a cool room","Sweating confined to the axillae with a family history of the same","Generalised sweating, nocturnal sweating, or an asymmetric distribution"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — primary disease is bilateral, focal and absent during sleep. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which molecular event is critical in actinic keratosis?","choices":["Overactivation of the hedgehog signalling pathway","Loss of keratinocyte adhesion through autoantibody binding","Reduced filaggrin production impairing the epidermal barrier","Ultraviolet-induced mutation of TP53"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the surrounding normal-looking skin harbours subclinical mutations, called field cancerisation. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A patient has had four episodes of herpes-associated erythema multiforme this year. Which chronic measure is appropriate?","choices":["Annual phototherapy given as a five-week course each spring","Suppressive antiviral therapy, such as aciclovir 400 mg twice daily","A standing prescription for oral prednisone to take at each episode","Long-term oral antihistamines taken continuously through the year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — preventing the herpes recurrence prevents the eruption. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which single feature most usefully distinguishes erythema nodosum from nodular vasculitis?","choices":["Erythema nodosum nodules are painless on palpation","Erythema nodosum nodules occur exclusively in men","Erythema nodosum nodules do not ulcerate","Erythema nodosum nodules appear only on the posterior legs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and they sit on the anterior shins whereas nodular vasculitis favours the posterior legs and ulcerates. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"How is an isolated actinic keratosis usually treated?","choices":["Photodynamic therapy using aminolevulinic acid and light","Cryotherapy with two to three freeze-thaw cycles","5-fluorouracil cream applied for two to four weeks","Imiquimod applied twice weekly for sixteen weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the standard of care for isolated lesions, with shave excision or curettage as alternatives. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"Which drug classes are classic causes of phototoxicity?","choices":["Aromatic anticonvulsants, allopurinol and nevirapine","Oral contraceptives, sulfonamides and bromides","Tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, amiodarone and thiazides","Sunscreen chemicals, sulfonamides and phenothiazines"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside furosemide, voriconazole, some nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and psoralens. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"How are Stevens-Johnson syndrome, the overlap state and toxic epidermal necrolysis separated?","choices":["By whether the Nikolsky sign is positive or negative at presentation","By body surface area of epidermal detachment: under 10%, 10 to 30%, and over 30%","By the presence of mucosal involvement, which occurs only in the severe form","By whether the trigger was a drug or an infection in each case"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the same disease spectrum divided by extent of detachment. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What single feature most reliably separates urticaria from its differentials?","choices":["Lesions appear symmetrically on the palms and soles","Lesions leave residual bruising as they resolve","Individual lesions last less than twenty-four hours","Individual lesions are intensely pruritic and blanch under pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — this is called the key diagnostic feature, and lesions persisting beyond it suggest urticarial vasculitis. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which feature separates papulopustular rosacea from acne vulgaris?","choices":["Rosacea has no comedones","Rosacea produces pustules and acne vulgaris does not","Rosacea occurs only in patients over the age of sixty","Rosacea spares the central face entirely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — open or closed comedones point to acne vulgaris instead. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which rosacea complication requires urgent ophthalmology referral?","choices":["Persistent central facial erythema unresponsive to topical therapy","Papules and pustules spreading beyond the central face","Ocular rosacea with keratitis, visual symptoms or corneal involvement","Rhinophyma causing nasal airway narrowing over several years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — untreated ocular disease risks corneal scarring. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 70"}]};
